# 3. Collections

A document read from JSON or YAML is `Data`: a tree of records, lists and
scalars. `Data` accepts any shape, so the checker knows little about what is
inside it. It cannot add up a `Data` list, because nothing says the elements
are numbers:

```hql
doc : Data = [412, 280, 310]
doc | sum
```

```text
check error[type-mismatch]: `sum` needs Collection<Int> or Collection<Float> or Collection<Decimal>, not Data
 --> <expression>:2:7
  |
2 | doc | sum
  |       ^^^
```

The same numbers written as a list are a `List<Int>`, and `sum` accepts it:

```hql
[412, 280, 310] | sum
```

```text
1002
```

A **collection** is a group of values of one type. Its type names the type of
its elements, so every step over it is checked: `sum` needs numbers, `sort`
needs values with an order, and `List<Int>` says the elements are `Int`.

## Lists, sets and maps

HQL has three kinds of collection. They differ in what they promise about
repeated values, order, and how you find an element:

| | `List<T>` | `Set<T>` | `Map<K, V>` |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Written | `[412, 280, 280]` | `{"sf", "novel"}` | `Map(["Dune"], [412])` |
| Repeated values | kept | removed | each key once |
| Order | by position, `0` to `size - 1` | none | none |
| Find an element | by position, `xs[0]` | by value, `contains` | by key, `get` |

A promise is part of the type, so the checker relies on it. A list has
positions, so `xs[0]` is allowed. A set has none, so a step that needs
positions is rejected before the program runs. A map's keys are unique, so a
lookup gives at most one value.

## The collection types

```d2
direction: down

abstract: "abstract: named in signatures, no values of their own" {
  direction: down
  style.fill: "#f4f4f4"
  style.stroke-dash: 3
  iterable: "Iterable<T>\nits elements can be visited"
  collection: "Collection<T>\nfinite; a value may occur\nmore than once"
  seq: "Seq<T>\nevery element has a position\n0 .. size - 1"
  iterable -> collection
  collection -> seq
}

values: "concrete: values you build" {
  direction: down
  style.fill: "#eef6ee"
  list: "List<T>\n[412, 280, 280]\npositions, repeats kept"
  set: "Set<T>\n{\"sf\", \"novel\"}\nno positions, no repeats"
  map: "Map<K, V>\nMap([\"Dune\"], [412])\nunique keys, no order"
  ordered: "OrderedMap<K, V>\nkeys in the order given"
  sorted: "SortedMap<K, V>\nkeys in the order of K"
  map -> ordered: "is a"
  ordered -> sorted: "is a"
}

abstract.seq -> values.list: "is a"
abstract.collection -> values.set: "is a"
```

The types at the top are **abstract**. They have no values of their own and
appear in the signatures of functions:

- `Iterable<T>` is anything whose elements can be visited one by one.
- `Collection<T>` is a finite group of elements in which a value may occur
  more than once. `size`, `count` and `contains` take any `Collection`.
- `Seq<T>` is a `Collection` in which every element has a position, from `0`
  to `size - 1`.

The types at the bottom are the ones you build. A `List<T>` is a `Seq<T>`. A
`Set<T>` is a `Collection<T>` with no repeated values and no positions. A
`Map<K, V>` is not a `Collection` at all: it associates keys with values, and
`OrderedMap` and `SortedMap` add an order to its keys.

A function that asks for a `Collection<Int>` accepts both a `List<Int>` and a
`Set<Int>`. A function that asks for a `Seq<Int>` accepts only the list.

## From Data to collections

Data from outside arrives as `Data` with no promises. A contract states what
the program expects of it, and a contract field can be a collection:

<!-- define: book-data -->
```hql
type BookData : DataContract = {
    title: String
    tags: List<String>
}
```

Reading a document as `BookData` checks every record, and every element of
each `tags` list. After that, the tags are a `List<String>` and the usual
steps apply to them:

<!-- run: with book-data -->
```hql
raw : List<Data> = [
    {title: "Dune", tags: ["desert", "spice"]},
    {title: "Ubik", tags: ["sf", "desert"]},
]
raw | BookData | flat_map(b => b.tags) | Set
```

```text
{"desert", "spice", "sf"}
```

`flat_map` collects the tags of every book into one list, and `Set` removes
the repeated `"desert"`. A tag that is not a `String` is rejected when the
document is read, and the error names the element by its path:

<!-- run: with book-data -->
```hql
raw : List<Data> = [{title: "Dune", tags: ["desert", 7]}]
raw | BookData | flat_map(b => b.tags) | Set
```

```text
execute error[contract-violation]: this does not read as BookData: field `tags.1`: 7 is not String
 --> <expression>:6:1
  |
6 | raw | BookData | flat_map(b => b.tags) | Set
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  = expected: BookData
```

```d2
direction: down

doc: "List<Data>\n[{title: \"Dune\", tags: [...]}, ...]\nany shape" {
  shape: page
  style.fill: "#f6efe6"
}

contract: "type BookData : DataContract = {\n  title: String\n  tags: List<String>\n}" {
  shape: hexagon
}

books: "List<BookData>\nevery record checked,\ntags included" {
  style.fill: "#eef6ee"
}

tags: "List<String>\nevery tag, repeats kept" {
  style.fill: "#eef6ee"
}

distinct: "Set<String>\nevery tag once" {
  style.fill: "#eef6ee"
}

rejected: "execute error\ncontract-violation\nfield `tags.1`: 7 is not String" {
  style.stroke: "#b23b3b"
}

doc -> contract: "| BookData"
contract -> books: "valid"
contract -> rejected: "invalid" {style.stroke: "#b23b3b"; style.stroke-dash: 5}
books -> tags: "| flat_map(b => b.tags)"
tags -> distinct: "| Set"
```

This is how a program works with data whose shape it does not control. It
leaves most of the document as `Data`, and names a contract for the part it
uses. Each step after the contract works on typed collections, and the
checker checks it.

Contracts nest. A field can be a list of another contract, and validation
follows the document all the way down:

```hql
type BookData : DataContract = {
    title: String
    pages: Int
}
type Shelf : DataContract = {
    books: List<BookData>
}

doc : Data = {
    owner: "ada",
    books: [{title: "Dune", pages: 412}, {title: "Emma", pages: "many"}],
}
doc | Shelf
```

```text
execute error[contract-violation]: this does not read as Shelf: field `books.1.pages`: "many" is not Int
  --> <expression>:13:1
   |
13 | doc | Shelf
   | ^^^^^^^^^^^
   = expected: Shelf
```

A JSON or YAML document has lists, but no sets and no maps. A JSON object is
a `Data` record, not a `Map`. A program builds sets and maps from the typed
lists a contract gives it, as the pages on [[sets|sets]] and [[maps|maps]]
show.

## Conversions are written

A value is never converted to another collection type implicitly. Binding a
list to a name declared as a set is a type error:

```hql
seen : Set<Int> = [1, 2]
seen
```

```text
check error[type-mismatch]: `seen` is declared Set<Int> but its value is List<Int>; a binding never converts, so construct it: `Set(…)`
 --> <expression>:1:19
  |
1 | seen : Set<Int> = [1, 2]
  |                   ^^^^^^
```

Each collection type has a **constructor** of the same name: `List(xs)`,
`Set(xs)` and `Map(keys, values)`. A constructor can also be written as a
step, because `x | T` is `T(x)`:

```hql
["novel", "essay", "novel", "poetry"] | Set | size
```

```text
3
```

[[steps|Steps on any collection]] shows every conversion between the three.

